<article class="env-lemma" id="069C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/069C">Lemma <span data-tag="069C">37.61.11</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a pseudo-coherent morphism of schemes. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is perfect, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathcal{O}_ X$ locally has finite tor dimension as a sheaf of $f^{-1}\mathcal{O}_ S$-modules, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for all $x \in X$ the ring $\mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ has finite tor dimension as an $\mathcal{O}_{S, f(x)}$-module. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The problem is local on $X$ and $S$. Hence we may assume that $X = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(B)$, $S = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$ and $f$ corresponds to a pseudo-coherent ring map $A \to B$. 
    <p>
      If (1) holds, then $B$ has finite tor dimension $d$ as $A$-module. Then $B_\mathfrak q$ has tor dimension $d$ as an $A_\mathfrak p$-module for all primes $\mathfrak q \subset B$ with $\mathfrak p = A \cap \mathfrak q$, see More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0B67" data-tag="0B67">15.68.15</a>. Then $\mathcal{O}_ X$ has tor dimension $d$ as a sheaf of $f^{-1}\mathcal{O}_ S$-modules by Cohomology, Lemma <a href="/tag/09U9" data-tag="09U9">20.48.5</a>. Thus (1) implies (2). 
    <p>
      By Cohomology, Lemma <a href="/tag/09U9" data-tag="09U9">20.48.5</a> (2) implies (3). 
    <p>
      Assume (3). We cannot use More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0B67" data-tag="0B67">15.68.15</a> to conclude as we are not given that the tor dimension of $B_\mathfrak q$ over $A_\mathfrak p$ is bounded independent of $\mathfrak q$. Choose a presentation $A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n] \to B$. Then $B$ is pseudo-coherent as a $A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$-module. Let $\mathfrak q \subset A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$ be a prime ideal lying over $\mathfrak p \subset A$. Then either $B_\mathfrak q$ is zero or by assumption it has finite tor dimension as an $A_\mathfrak p$-module. Since the fibres of $A \to A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$ have finite global dimension, we can apply More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/068X" data-tag="068X">15.79.5</a> to $A_\mathfrak p \to A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]_\mathfrak q$ to see that $B_\mathfrak q$ is a perfect $A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]_\mathfrak q$-module. Hence $B$ is a perfect $A[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]$-module by More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/068W" data-tag="068W">15.79.3</a>. Thus $A \to B$ is a perfect ring map by definition. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>